WEBVTT

00:06.604 --> 00:14.769
[SPEAKER_00]: What is up everyone? Welcome back to Paragliding Atlas podcast. I am Anindar Singh and if you are new here, this is the show where we talk about

00:14.989 --> 00:24.015
[SPEAKER_00]: all things paragliding. From gear tips to flying techniques and stories to experiences. We cover some of the most interesting and

00:24.055 --> 00:32.000
[SPEAKER_00]: important topics that you have always wanted to know more about from the blue yonder. Today, however, I want to chat about something that is

00:32.080 --> 00:42.007
[SPEAKER_00]: crucial for all of us who love to fly. And I'm talking about none other than our humble carabiners. You know, those small metal clips that keep

00:42.047 --> 00:50.853
[SPEAKER_00]: us connected to our wings, which we rely on every time we take to the skies? But how much do we really know about them? Let's take a closer look

00:51.493 --> 01:00.434
[SPEAKER_00]: and see how we can make sure that they are safe and reliable as we practice the art of flight. So as I was designing this episode, I reached out to a

01:00.594 --> 01:08.701
[SPEAKER_00]: trustworthy equipment manufacturer in our industry, which goes by the name of Finsterwalder and Charlie. Now, Finsterwalder and Charlie

01:08.741 --> 01:17.906
[SPEAKER_00]: have often been known to make high quality carabiners for various applications in the sport of free flights over many years. But Thomas

01:17.946 --> 01:25.172
[SPEAKER_00]: Finsterwalder, the owner of the company himself, could not be a part of the show due to his limited English language proficiency. However, he

01:25.192 --> 01:32.579
[SPEAKER_00]: was kind enough to send me a research paper that he had worked upon a few years ago from which I was able to take some very key takeaways and

01:32.639 --> 01:41.368
[SPEAKER_00]: prepare the first of its kind solo episode for all of you out there. Now, even though I completely understand that the world of carabiner is much

01:41.468 --> 01:50.672
[SPEAKER_00]: larger to cover in a single episode, this, however, is an attempt to cover one of the most important, yet one of the most overlooked topics of

01:50.752 --> 01:57.795
[SPEAKER_00]: material fatigue. Today we'll try to simplify some technical concepts, share some insights from the paragliding community,

01:58.455 --> 02:06.118
[SPEAKER_00]: discuss some practical maintenance tips, and highlight why it is so important to pay attention to these small but vital pieces of

02:06.178 --> 02:10.320
[SPEAKER_00]: equipment. So without further ado, let's get started.

02:14.522 --> 02:21.146
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's begin this episode by acknowledging that there have been some incidents in our community where carabiner failures have often led to

02:21.266 --> 02:29.230
[SPEAKER_00]: serious accidents. While specific details are often kept confidential out of the respect for those involved, these unfortunate

02:29.370 --> 02:37.052
[SPEAKER_00]: events serve as a stark remainder of the importance of regular equipment inspection and maintenance. And material fatigue and

02:37.092 --> 02:45.016
[SPEAKER_00]: corrosion have often been identified as contributing factors in some of these cases. Most of the time they involve carabiners which are used

02:45.176 --> 02:54.201
[SPEAKER_00]: beyond their recommended service life or without proper inspection and care. These incidents underscore the critical need to understand

02:54.381 --> 03:04.802
[SPEAKER_00]: how and why carabiners fail even when they appear to be in a perfectly good condition upon visual inspection. So what exactly is material

03:04.842 --> 03:15.266
[SPEAKER_00]: fatigue and why is it so crucial for us as pilots? To explain this further, I would want you to imagine a paperclip, something as simple as

03:15.326 --> 03:24.771
[SPEAKER_00]: that. And if you keep on bending a paperclip back and forth repeatedly, what really happens is that initial few times, it flexes without any

03:24.811 --> 03:33.293
[SPEAKER_00]: problem. but over time it keeps on becoming weaker and weaker at a certain point and eventually snaps. Now this is the simplest way to

03:33.373 --> 03:42.435
[SPEAKER_00]: visualize material fatigue. The progressive structural damage that occurs when a material is subjected to repeated stress cycles. In

03:42.495 --> 03:50.977
[SPEAKER_00]: context of paragliding, every single launch, every single encounter of turbulence, or when we perform maneuvers, our carabiners are kind of

03:51.057 --> 04:00.060
[SPEAKER_00]: going through the same stress cycles. Now, applying the same analogy of paperclip, once these stress cycles keep on repeating themselves over

04:00.100 --> 04:07.836
[SPEAKER_00]: a period of time, they tend to cause microscopic cracks in the metal. Now, I understand visualizing microscopic cracks is going to be

04:07.876 --> 04:16.301
[SPEAKER_00]: difficult, but the problem is while individual stresses may be well within the carabiner's capacity or the rated limit, the cumulative

04:16.421 --> 04:24.966
[SPEAKER_00]: effect over a period of time can weaken its strength significantly. And that is why understanding material fatigue and how it affects our

04:25.006 --> 04:31.110
[SPEAKER_00]: equipment is very essential to our safety. Now, as I was going through the research paper, I thought of having some conversation with some

04:31.150 --> 04:37.855
[SPEAKER_00]: experienced pilots who shared their thoughts on this topic. One of the pilots mentioned in their early days they didn't really pay much

04:37.895 --> 04:46.284
[SPEAKER_00]: attention to carabiners. They assumed, just like all of us, that as long as the equipment wasn't visibly damaged, it was fairly safe to use. And

04:46.384 --> 04:55.051
[SPEAKER_00]: another pilot who's an acrobatic flyer pointed out that high stress maneuvers actually put a lot more additional stress on carabiners. And

04:55.251 --> 05:02.317
[SPEAKER_00]: in fact, acro flyers make it a point to replace their carabiners more frequently than the usual cross-country flyer or the weekend flyers.

05:03.418 --> 05:10.363
[SPEAKER_00]: And most importantly, they also try and keep a log of their flights and maneuvers to monitor the stresses placed on their equipment over a

05:10.403 --> 05:19.449
[SPEAKER_00]: period of time. Now these insights highlight a common realization amongst a lot of us and that is even without visible signs of wear,

05:20.050 --> 05:27.915
[SPEAKER_00]: carabiners can fail if not properly replaced or maintained when necessary. To illustrate this further, I would like to break down a

05:27.955 --> 05:37.739
[SPEAKER_00]: technical concept and I'll try and keep it as straightforward as possible. Let's talk about static strength. Static strength refers to

05:37.779 --> 05:46.724
[SPEAKER_00]: the maximum load a carabiner can withstand when a force is applied in steady and slow manner. In other words, application of a constant and

05:46.964 --> 05:56.830
[SPEAKER_00]: unchanging load. For example, if a carabiner, as they usually are, is rated at 20 kN. If you look at any of your carabiner from your equipment,

05:56.850 --> 06:06.810
[SPEAKER_00]: you can see it must be rated at 18 or 16 or 20 kAmp. Now, kilonewton is a unit of measurement which runs in thousands. So let's say 20 kilonewton

06:06.850 --> 06:17.299
[SPEAKER_00]: means 2000 kilograms. And the carabiner is rated to not fail up till 2000 kilograms under static conditions. However, this varies differently

06:17.499 --> 06:25.345
[SPEAKER_00]: in real life flying conditions when we are actually dealing with dynamic loads. And these forces vary greatly in magnitude and

06:25.385 --> 06:33.272
[SPEAKER_00]: direction, and often suddenly at times. You know, visualize this. Think about hitting a turbulence and facing a massive collapse. And

06:33.292 --> 06:41.058
[SPEAKER_00]: then the reopening is so violent that sometimes these forces on the carabiner can spike momentarily, exceeding the typical load limits

06:41.158 --> 06:48.766
[SPEAKER_00]: experiencing during the regular flight. Now going back to our paperclip analogy, bending it gently in one direction may not cause any

06:48.846 --> 06:58.133
[SPEAKER_00]: issues. But rapidly bending it back and forth accelerates the fatigue process. So similarly, dynamic loads also contribute to the material

06:58.173 --> 07:08.139
[SPEAKER_00]: fatigue in carabiners in a very same way. Each stress cycle, even if it's below the carabiner's maximum rating, adds up over time. And this

07:08.259 --> 07:16.102
[SPEAKER_00]: cumulative effect can weaken the carabiner, eventually making it more susceptible to failing. Now, manufacturers of paragliding equipment

07:16.342 --> 07:25.008
[SPEAKER_00]: such as Petzl and Edelrid provide guidelines to help keep our gear in top condition. While specifics may slightly vary depending upon the model

07:25.068 --> 07:33.836
[SPEAKER_00]: and the equipment number you have, there are some common themes which we can focus on. Firstly, the service life is a very crucial concept. Even

07:33.876 --> 07:41.839
[SPEAKER_00]: though metallic equipment doesn't degrade in the same way as a fabric or a plastic in paragliding, the manufacturers recommend retiring

07:41.880 --> 07:50.043
[SPEAKER_00]: carabiners after a certain period of time or the amount of use. And factors such as frequency of use and types of activities and

07:50.163 --> 07:59.208
[SPEAKER_00]: environmental conditions can all influence this timeframe greatly. Secondly, regular inspections are essential. Before and after each

07:59.248 --> 08:08.936
[SPEAKER_00]: flight we should check our carabiners for any signs of wear, deformation or corrosion or damage. There is often emphasis on proper

08:08.976 --> 08:15.541
[SPEAKER_00]: maintenance and this basically in simpler words means cleaning the carabiner after they have been exposed to something out of the ordinary

08:16.041 --> 08:23.928
[SPEAKER_00]: such as dirt or sand or salt water which comes down to drying them thoroughly before storage or lubricating the moving parts with

08:24.008 --> 08:32.752
[SPEAKER_00]: appropriate products such as silicon based lubricants. Finally, the storage also matters up to a certain point, although this goes overall

08:33.032 --> 08:41.435
[SPEAKER_00]: for the harnesses in general, but keeping carabiners along with the harness in a cool and dry place, away from direct sunlight, not leaving

08:41.475 --> 08:50.138
[SPEAKER_00]: them in your car or out in the landing field for long will of course help extend the lifetime of our equipment and eventually enhance the safety

08:50.878 --> 08:58.454
[SPEAKER_00]: in our skies while we practice the out-of-flight. Now, as we are talking about manufacturer recommendations, I would also like to bring in a

08:58.494 --> 09:07.479
[SPEAKER_00]: detail about environmental factors and their impact on our equipment. Pilots who frequently fly in coastal areas or humid climates need to be

09:07.619 --> 09:15.844
[SPEAKER_00]: particularly vigilant about saltwater, as the moist air can accelerate this corrosion and even on the metals that are generally

09:15.884 --> 09:25.507
[SPEAKER_00]: resistant to rust. So for example, if you're flying around the coast, carabiners are exposed to salty, humid air, and this can eventually

09:25.567 --> 09:33.432
[SPEAKER_00]: lead to corrosion if not cleaned properly. So it's very important to rinse carabiners with fresh water after such flights and dry them

09:33.512 --> 09:41.236
[SPEAKER_00]: thoroughly before storage. And same goes for sandy or dusty environments which can cause abrasion and wear and tear eventually. So

09:41.376 --> 09:49.681
[SPEAKER_00]: overall regular cleaning helps remove small particles that might grind against the moving parts or surfaces and cause more damage to

09:49.721 --> 09:56.745
[SPEAKER_00]: these microscopic cracks that eventually develop while we are practicing some hygiene maneuvers. Although we might not always think

09:56.785 --> 10:04.270
[SPEAKER_00]: about environmental factors but they do play a significant role in longevity and reliability of our equipment so being mindful of these

10:04.310 --> 10:12.269
[SPEAKER_00]: conditions and taking appropriate care can make a big difference. Now moving on, I would like to address some common misconceptions that can

10:12.309 --> 10:20.316
[SPEAKER_00]: often lead to complacency. The first one, which of course even I as a flyer have to go through a lot of time is that if it looks fine, it is

10:20.416 --> 10:29.003
[SPEAKER_00]: usually safe. You know, because usually we visually inspect something in the first go and it's easy to assume if our carabiners show no visible

10:29.083 --> 10:37.766
[SPEAKER_00]: signs of damage, they're kind of good to go. However, as talked about previously, the damage in carabiners happens at a microscopic level

10:38.447 --> 10:45.978
[SPEAKER_00]: and isn't always visible during visual inspection except for the usual paint job or some bit of scratches that we might be able to watch out for.

10:47.105 --> 10:55.150
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, oftentimes I've seen, and this usually goes for people buying pre-owned equipment, that carabiners last forever if they're not

10:55.270 --> 11:03.195
[SPEAKER_00]: involved in an accident. And needless to say, as mentioned earlier, regular stresses will accumulate over a period of time based on how much

11:03.316 --> 11:13.502
[SPEAKER_00]: the equipment has been used by the previous owner. So carabiners do have a finite service life and using them beyond this period will increase

11:13.522 --> 11:21.365
[SPEAKER_00]: the risk of failure greatly. Now third one and the last one is basically about steel carabiners because steel is much more durable and

11:21.425 --> 11:29.150
[SPEAKER_00]: resistant to wear and tear than aluminium that is more common in our industry. However, we have to understand it is not immune to material

11:29.190 --> 11:37.595
[SPEAKER_00]: fatigue and corrosion and they also require regular inspection and maintenance just like the other aluminium or alloy carabiners that we

11:37.635 --> 11:46.702
[SPEAKER_00]: are most commonly using in our usual equipment. Now let's get on to some actionable steps that we all can take to make sure that we keep our

11:46.722 --> 11:54.968
[SPEAKER_00]: equipment in top shape possible. One of the simplest things that comes to my mind is aluminum carabiners have to be replaced every five years

11:55.128 --> 12:03.855
[SPEAKER_00]: according to manufacturer's recommendation, especially if you fly frequently. And on top of that, if you engage in acro flying or perform

12:03.915 --> 12:12.118
[SPEAKER_00]: dynamic maneuvers such as wing overs or spiral dives, you might consider replacing them a bit more often. Thorough inspections also

12:12.198 --> 12:21.043
[SPEAKER_00]: help a lot. Visual checks before every flight such as figuring out if the gate is opening or looking for cracks or deformation of corrosion helps

12:21.103 --> 12:31.770
[SPEAKER_00]: a lot. However, detailed inspections periodically can take this a step further. Try taking a closer look using a magnifying glass or even

12:31.830 --> 12:40.435
[SPEAKER_00]: better try having a professional qualified person inspect your gear. Now I can sum up this section that by incorporating these simple tips

12:40.475 --> 12:48.244
[SPEAKER_00]: into your routine you can help ensure that your equipment remains reliable and safe and you can have the peace of mind that you want while

12:48.264 --> 12:55.693
[SPEAKER_00]: you take to the skies. So staying updated with latest information from manufacturers and paragliding community is vital. Because

12:55.733 --> 13:04.058
[SPEAKER_00]: manufacturers often release safety notices or updates based on their guidelines on new research and new findings. So subscribing to their

13:04.118 --> 13:11.042
[SPEAKER_00]: newsletters or checking their websites periodically can help keep you informed. Additionally, engaging with fellow pilots through clubs,

13:11.142 --> 13:18.906
[SPEAKER_00]: forums or social media also allows us to share experiences and learn from each other. And remember, knowledge is a powerful tool in

13:18.986 --> 13:28.585
[SPEAKER_00]: enhancing our safety and the safety of people around us. Now, as I plan to wrap up this episode, I would like to talk a little bit about advancement

13:28.625 --> 13:37.109
[SPEAKER_00]: in carabiner technologies and how it looks forward in time to come. And how it looks in time to come. Oftentimes, manufacturers are always

13:37.169 --> 13:45.892
[SPEAKER_00]: exploring new alloys and composite material that offer high strength to weight ratios and build resistance to fatigue. These eventually aim

13:45.932 --> 13:54.298
[SPEAKER_00]: to produce carabiners that are lighter and yet more durable. The gate mechanisms have been there for a while and in time to come the idea is to

13:54.358 --> 14:01.004
[SPEAKER_00]: reduce gate flutter and help minimize the risk of accidental gate openings and eventually reduce the stress on carabiners during

14:01.064 --> 14:11.274
[SPEAKER_00]: dynamic loads. Some carabiners are also planning to bring in the visual cues of featuring wear indicators which basically will alert you when

14:11.294 --> 14:19.237
[SPEAKER_00]: the equipment is reaching its end of its service life or needs inspection. Although still in development, there are some talks about

14:19.497 --> 14:27.861
[SPEAKER_00]: RFID tags or sensors into carabiners to try and track the usage and stress cycle. This technology one day could provide pilots with

14:27.941 --> 14:36.045
[SPEAKER_00]: real-time data on their equipment and conditions. While these innovations sound really exciting, I would like to undermine that they

14:36.205 --> 14:43.667
[SPEAKER_00]: do not replace the need for regular maintenance and responsible equipment management. So to bring it all together, here are some steps

14:44.067 --> 14:51.452
[SPEAKER_00]: that you can take starting today. First of all, check the age and condition of your carabiners. Review your equipment. Refer to the

14:51.492 --> 15:00.398
[SPEAKER_00]: manufacturer's guidelines for service and maintenance life. Secondly, educate yourself. Read the user manuals and safety notices

15:00.438 --> 15:08.623
[SPEAKER_00]: from your manufacturer. Stay informed about new developments and recommendations in the paragliding community. Thirdly, implement a

15:08.703 --> 15:16.729
[SPEAKER_00]: maintenance routine. Make this a part of your lifestyle. Do regular inspections and try and stick to them. Try and keep a logbook of your

15:16.829 --> 15:24.601
[SPEAKER_00]: equipment's usage and maintenance performed as well, and not just about the flying hours. And lastly, engaging with the community,

15:25.121 --> 15:33.930
[SPEAKER_00]: sharing your experiences, participating in discussions, workshops, and webinars will help a lot. And taking these proactive steps, you not

15:33.990 --> 15:40.376
[SPEAKER_00]: only enhance your own safety, but also contribute to the culture of responsibility and awareness in the paragliding community as a whole.

15:41.417 --> 15:49.284
[SPEAKER_00]: So, final thoughts to wrap this up. Material fatigue in carabiners is a very crucial issue that deserves our attention. While it might seem

15:49.404 --> 15:57.610
[SPEAKER_00]: technical or even daunting at first, understanding the basics empowers us to make better decisions about our equipment. Remember,

15:58.450 --> 16:07.773
[SPEAKER_00]: these little small pieces of metal are responsible for keeping us connected to our wing and keeping us safe. So respecting their

16:07.813 --> 16:16.775
[SPEAKER_00]: limitations, maintaining them properly and replacing them when needed is the way we can entrust our lives to them in a responsible

16:16.815 --> 16:24.392
[SPEAKER_00]: manner. And on that note, thank you so much for joining me on this journey into the world of carabiner safety. I hope you found this discussion

16:24.432 --> 16:30.955
[SPEAKER_00]: insightful and it inspires you to take a closer look at your own gear. If you have any questions, thoughts or experiences that you'd like to

